\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Web applications and the problems commonly faced by developers extend across many or all of these levels.
\par 
\par Any framework for describing patterns in web programming will need to be capable of explaining the design considerations across any or all of these levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 3.1.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Common Problems and Techniques
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Form Processing
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Form processing is used to capture the problem of getting and verifying input from a user.  It is at the core of all web applications and there are many different approaches for solving this problem available to the web developer.
\par 
\par The basic method and constructs for prompting the user remain the same regardless of the actual content being entered.  All forms must:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls4\adjustright {
Display an empty form to the user
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls4\adjustright {
Verify the data entered is valid and display an error if it is incorrect
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls4\adjustright {
Perform the required action using the data
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Forms can be implem
ented using either a single or multiple page design.  The single page technique utilizes a self-referring script to display and process the form.  Multi-page forms take the user through a series of pages to enter, confirm and submit the data.
\par 
\par The other im
portant area to consider when thinking about form design is the reporting mechanism for invalid data.  Some people like to display the form with the input highlighting any errors.  This makes it simple for the user to find and fix problems in the data.  O
t
hers prefer to keep the page and application design as simple as possible by reporting errors on a separate page.  The user then needs to either go back to the original form using the back button on their browser or they may be presented with a link to a 
page which will contain their entered data.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {\page Navigation
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Clean, structured navigation and information design is one of the most important aspects of web design.  There needs to be a consistent look and feel throughout the site.  Users need to be able to imm
ediately recognize their location in the site as a search engine referral can throw them anywhere.
\par 
\par There are three major navigation designs employed on the web today.  Single level navigation has a list of top level areas within the site.
\par 
\par Multi-level nav
igation breaks the site into a hierarchy.  The user can then drill down through these levels to find the desired content.  All levels of the hierarchy are displayed as part of the navigation at all times.  As such the hierarchy is usually only two levels 
deep to keep the display simple.
\par 
\par Dynamic multi-level navigation also uses a hierarchy except here the next level of navigation is not shown to the user until they have selected the parent.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Database Operations
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par How to best access a database is a fundamental problem for web developers.  In fact, it\rquote 
s so common that we could easily forget to consider it as being a problem we need to solve.  Most people access databases through specific functions or an abstracted database wrapper.
\par 
\par Many web applications and forms revolve around letting the user insert new information into the database and edit existing entries.  The data being used is irrelevant to the solutions employed making this a good candidate for a pattern.
\par 
\par If we examine data entry at a higher level many objects / items we use in web programming are at their core captured by a row in the database.  Creating a set of useful functions and information based on these items is a common problem for web developers.

\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Authentication
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Any request to a web site is authenticated at one of the following levels:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls6\adjustright {None \endash 
 we do nothing to record or track the request
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls6\adjustright {Session \endash 
 we track this anonymous person through their current visit only
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls6\adjustright {Visitor \endash 
 we track this anonymous person across numerous sessions at the site
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls6\adjustright {User \endash 
 we have information about the person and require them to authenticate
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par These levels of authentication are related and have similar implementations.  They should be considered together as we need to know the authentication level of any request to the site.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Error Handling
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Building graceful degradation into a web site is a great challenge.  Catching errors before they confuse users is vital.  Logging and notifying the maintainers of these errors is equally important to prevent them happening again.
\par 
\par Another
 good error handling problem to consider is keeping the site alive even if components are off-line.  For example, if the database is being backed up and thus read-only can we continue to serve database requests without trying to write.  Or if the database
 is offline completely can we continue to show pages that do not really need to use the database.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.3.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {E-commerce
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par E-commerce is an interesting problem because it requires the form and authentication problems above to be solved.  The challenge is to build a pattern for e-commerce that is not dependent on a particular implementation of forms or authentication.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 3.1.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {
Categorizing the Application Levels affected by each Problem
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Introduction
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Placing problems into their levels will help us to understand which problems are specific to web programming.  For example, problems that fit solely into the library code layer are probably best described using an existing design pattern language.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Form Processing
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par All form processing is done through a user interface interaction with the user.  This results in calls to the high-level functions implemented in the code libraries.  As such, form processing is limited to all of the front-end levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Navigation
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Single and multi-level navigation is limited to the user interface and human computer interaction levels.
\par 
\par Dynamic multi-level navigation requires knowledge of the users current location and the ability to generate navigational menus.  Backend libraries will also be required to generate these menus.  Whether these should be part of
 a pattern for multi-level navigation or just listed as a collaborator is open to debate.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Database Operations
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Database wrapper code lives in the code library and database query levels.  It actually only needs to know about simple database queries since most are just passed through the wrapper class.
\par 
\par Formalizing the process of inserting and editing data into the database requires the use of all levels.  We need to design the database to facilitate this type of data entry.  The user interface must be developed to make the process clear.
\par 
\par Trying to encapsulate database rows as objects or items is limited to the backend levels.  The front-end has access to this data through the API developed for the items.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Authentication
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par User level authentication requires interaction with the person through the front-end.  It also requires backend functions to verify and process the information arising from this interaction.  It uses all levels of web programming.
\par 
\par All other forms of authentication require no interaction with the user.  They are completely contained in the backend levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Error Handling
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par Errors must be reported to the user and detected at all levels of the application.  Error handling code uses all levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.1.4.7\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {E-commerce
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par E-commerce requires interaction with the user and backend processing of the data.  It requires all levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\i\f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls2\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid {\page De
sign Patterns in the Context of Web Programming
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4.1.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Choosing an Appropriate Level of Abstraction
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par I spent a lot of time thinking about what would be some example web programming design patterns.  I knew that these patterns should describe the techniques I\rquote 
d learnt over time but I also felt that the patterns should be as generic and abstract as possible.
\par 
\par My confusion arose because I did not choose an appropriate level of abstraction.  Web
 programming is built on top of the principles of programming in general.  As I tried to abstract the patterns out they ended up becoming patterns appropriate for generic programming, they were less obvious and useful when applied to the web programming d
omain.
\par 
\par Web programming is a very specific area.  Our aim is to develop patterns that are useful in the context of that domain.  Patterns should describe the high level concepts that are used in the chosen domain.  Thus, in our case the developed patterns w
ill also be fairly specific.
\par 
\par Let\rquote s consider an example to illustrate this point.  Object oriented programming can be used in any domain.  The patterns developed need to be a level of abstraction away from this method of programming.  So, these patterns de
scribe ways to solve problems in object oriented programming.  Our patterns need to describe ways to solve problems in web development.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4.1.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Application Levels
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par I originally felt that there was no need to consider design patterns for problems that fell into areas with existing documented patterns.  For example, a database abstraction class really falls into the object-oriented realm.
\par 
\par While it\rquote s important to think about application levels when describing patterns, we should ignore them when choosing patterns to describe.  We are looking for patterns relevant to web programming, whatever their application level.
\par 
\par The question then remains as to whether we should describe patterns that fall into certain application levels.  Should the database abstraction class above be d
escribed using the object oriented design pattern language or our web programming design pattern language?
\par 
\par Again the answer lies in our viewpoint.  We are describing patterns from the viewpoint of a web developer.  Our patterns need a suitable level of ab
straction from that starting point.  The object-oriented design pattern language was created to help people describe abstract concepts about classes, methods and their interaction.  Our database abstraction pattern needs to provide more specifics than thi
s language may allow.
\par 
\par Our web programming design pattern language should allow us to describe patterns that fall across any of the application levels.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\i\f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls2\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid {A Design Pattern Language for Web Programming
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Basis
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {This language is a modified version of that presented for object oriented programming on page 6 of Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software by Gamma et al.
\par 
\par In the interests of reuse, I have reproduced their clear descriptions of the pattern language elements where appropriate below.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Definition
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Pattern Name and Classification
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {The pattern\rquote s name conveys the essence of the pattern succinctly.  A good name is vital, because it will become part of your design vocabulary.  The pattern\rquote 
s classification reflects the scheme introduced in the next section.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Intent
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {A short statement that answers the following questions: What does the design pattern do?  What is its rationale and intent?  What particular design issue or problem does it address?
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Also Known As
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Other well-known names for the pattern, if any.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Motivation
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {An example describing the function and use of the pattern.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Applicability
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {What are the situations where this design pattern can be applied?  How can you recognize these situations?
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Sequence of Events
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {A step by step description of how the pattern ex
ecutes.  This should describe the high level steps, not implementation specific details.  Take particular care to document the sequence of HTTP requests.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.7\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Participants
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {The patterns and / or classes participating in the design pattern and their responsibilities.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.8\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Collaborations
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {How the participants collaborate to carry out their responsibilities.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.9\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Consequences
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {How does the pattern support its objectives?  What are the trade-offs and results of using the pattern?
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.10\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Implementation
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {What pitfalls, hints, or techniques should you be aware of when implementing the pattern?  Are there language-specific issues?
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.11\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Sample Code
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Code fragments that illustrate how you might implement the pattern in PHP, Java or your favorite web programming language.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.1.2.12\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Related Patterns
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {What design patterns are closely related to this one?  What are the important differences?  With which other patterns should this one be used?
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\i\f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls2\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid {Web Programming Design Pattern Space
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par As the catalogue of design patterns grows we need a way to organize them.  This helps us improve our understanding of patterns and their effects.
\par 
\par Behavioral patterns describe the way a web application proceeds to perform a given task.
\par 
\par Structural patterns describe an event sequence for web programs.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\i\f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls2\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid {Example Patterns
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7.1.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Filter
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Classification
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Behavioural
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Intent
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Provide a mechanism for conditionally executing code at the start or end of each page request.
\par }{
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Motivation}{
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Having the ability to conditionally run functions as part of a web page is a very powerful tool for web programmers.  It gives us the flexibility to add and configure logging functions, a
uthentication checks and so on.  Filters control the registration and execution of these functions.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Applicability
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Filters can be used to execute functions at the beginning or end of a script based on certain conditions.  These conditions may include things like:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls11\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls11\adjustright {The requested URL
 matching a given regular expression
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls11\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls11\adjustright {
A random chance factor (eg: run the function 50% of the time)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par A good example of using filters for logging would be to have some sample of requests being logged.  This is achieved by setting an appropriate random chance factor.
\par 
\par Another model use of a filter is to add code allowing feedback to all pages matching a given regular expression.  For example, you might want to give users the ability to add comments to your editor
ials, but not your articles.  Just add a filter with the appropriate URL regular expression.
\par 
\par Need to add a footer to every page on your site?  Just use a filter.
\par 
\par }{Filters can be named.  Named filters are useful when you want to have cascading filter permissions.  For example, }{if you want to have authentication levels setup on your site.  Imagine placing a User authentication filter to be executed for all
 pages.  Sections of the site that are to be visible to anyone now need to be explicitly declared that way by registering functions.  We could add a Visitor authentication filter that returns filter_break_named on the /public folder for example.  
On the public folder both filters will be executed.  But the Visitor authentication filter is registe
red first so it will run and then the filter_break_named will prevent anymore filters with that name being run so the User authentication filter will be ignored.}{
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Sequence of Events
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright \cgrid {Client requests page}{

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright {Register filters
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright {
Check conditions on all post_auth filters and run if met
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright {Run page script
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 5.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls12\adjustright {
Check conditions on all trace filters and run if met
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Participants}{
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.7\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Collaborations}{
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {The developer may register and run a number of post_auth and / or trace filters.  The return values of these filter functions influence the continuing execution of the filters.  See the 
Implementation section below for more information.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.8\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Consequences
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard\plain \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls13\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls13\adjustright \cgrid {Configuring 
conditional code execution is easy.  Adding a new logging function or authentication filter is just a filter registration function call away.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls13\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls13\adjustright {Be careful of filter side effects.  
Filters can stop the rest of a page from completing execution or stop other filters from running.  You need to pay special attention to the order that filters are registered and their likely return values.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.9\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Implementation
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Filters are run before the page script (post_auth) or after it has completed (trace).
\par 
\par Developers should be able to register any function to be triggered by a filter.  The registration should specify the conditions that must be met (or not met) for the filter to be triggered.  Registration should give developers 
the ability to pass variables to the registered function at the time it is run.
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {The filter function must return one of three values:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls14\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls14\adjustright {filter_ok
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls14\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls14\adjustright {filter_break}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls14\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls14\adjustright {filter_break_named}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls14\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls14\adjustright {filter_return
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par The meanings of these values in a postauth filter are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls15\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls15\adjustright {
filter_ok - continue as normal, including the processing of other}{ }{matching postauth filters.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls15\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls15\adjustright {
filter_break - do not process any more postauth filters, but continue}{ }{processing the request}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls15\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls15\adjustright {filter_break_named - do 
not process any more filters with the same name as the current filter}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls15\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls15\adjustright {
filter_return - do not process any more postauth filters, do not}{ process the request.  t}{race filters are run.  It is assumed that}{ }{the filter has returned a proper response to the client.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par The meanings of these values in a trace filter are:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls16\adjustright {
filter_ok - continue as normal, including the processing of other}{ }{matching trace filters.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls16\adjustright {filter_break - do not}{
 process any more trace filters
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls16\adjustright {filter_break_named - do 
not process any more filters wi}{th the same name as the current }{filter
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 -\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls16\pnrnot0\pnstart25\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb -}}\ls16\adjustright {filter_return - do not process
 any more trace filters}{
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.10\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Sample Code
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par }{ss_register_filter ($when, $method, $url_pattern, $run_on_match, $function, }{
\par }\pard \li720\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {$arg1 = ss_ignore_this_arg, }{
\par }{$arg2 = ss_ignore_this_arg, }{
\par }{$arg3 = ss_ignore_this_arg, }{
\par }{$arg4 = ss_ignore_this_arg, }{
\par }\pard \li720\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {$arg5 = ss_ignore_this_arg)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {Registers a filter to be run when the parameters are matched.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {$when can be postauth or trace.  A postauth filter is run before the
\par code to generate the page is executed.  postauth refers to the fact
\par that this filter is being run after the user has been authenticated
\par by the web server (not by the system).  A trace filter is run after
\par the page has been generated.  Ideally, it would be after the page has
\par been returned to the user, but Apache/PHP can't do that.
\par 
\par $method is GET, POST or ANY.  This allows filters to be applied to only
\par certain types of requests.
\par 
\par If $run_on_match is true then the filter is run only when the request
\par uri matches $url_pattern.   If $run_on_match is false then the filter
\par is run when the request uri does not match any of the reg exp's in
\par $url_pattern.
\par 
\par Note that $url_pattern can be any regular expression.  Multiple filters
\par can be run for the same request if there are multiple matches.  You may
\par also specify a set for $url_pattern.  This should be a set of strings
\par which are all the reg exps that this filter is to be used for.  If you
\par specify a set of reg exps for the filter then you should be careful that
\par they do not overlap.  For example if you give Set('.*', 'foo*') the filter
\par will be run twice for any url that matches foo*.  One easy way to avoid
\par this is to give the filter a name and use filter_break_named as the return
\par value from the filter function.
\par 
\par If this filter is triggered then $function is run and sent the list
\par of arguments.  Any argument that is set to ss_ignore_this_arg is not sent
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {to the function.  This allows up to five arguments to be defined.}{
\par }{
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.1.11\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Related Patterns
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {None.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7.1.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Other Behavioral Patterns
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.2.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Data Entry
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {This pattern is used to describe a form sequence that facilitates the entry of new data into the database.  The data is checked for validity before being entered.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.2.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Data Edit
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {Data Edit is a pattern describing the form sequence for editing data that already exists in the database.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.2.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Data Maintenance
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {We can combine the ideas of Data Entry and Data Edit into a single pattern Data Maintenance which shares forms and checking code to allow both insertions and editing.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.2.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Authentication
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {This pattern describes an interface that abstracts the authentication process from its caller.  This allows us to easily switch between authentication schemes or change the site security at any time.

\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.2.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Error Handling
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {The Error Handling pattern describes a process for dealing with errors.  Users should be given a suitable message and the site maintainer notified.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7.1.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\fi-720\li720\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls2\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \f1\cgrid {Structural Patterns
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.3.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Self-Referring Page
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {This pattern describes the layout and execution process for a form that has the display elements, validation code and 
final operations all contained in a single script.  The action element for the form refers back to itself.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s4 \b\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 7.1.3.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s4\fi-864\li864\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx864\ls2\ilvl3\outlinelevel3\adjustright \b\cgrid {Multiple Page Form
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {The Mulitple Page Form pattern describes the optimal layout for form display, validation code and final operations across a sequence of pages.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\i\f1\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 8\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa60\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls2\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid {Future Work
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {
\par This catalogue of design patterns is by no means complete or even well written.  Perfecting the description of these patterns is an ongoing process that will take a lot of time and collaboration to complete.
\par 
\par No doubt as the catalogue expands so will our understanding of what is required to adequately describe a web programming design pattern.  I would expect the design pattern language to evolve and mature over time.
\par }}